}^{2}}{j}\right)\), we use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the form:           
  \[                                                                            
  \left(\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{j} b_{j}\right)^{2} \leq \left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}         
a_{j}^{2}\right) \left(\sum_{j=1}^{n} b_{j}^{2}\right).                         
  \]                                                                            
  This inequality holds because the square of any real number is non-negative,  
and the product of sums of squares is also non-negative. Additionally, we can   
apply the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the context of sums of ratios to obtain  
the desired result.                                                             
